comments.body:
Rebased onto `main` — this one had real conflicts, so flagging exactly what I changed while resolving them. Your commit and authorship are intact; the two edits below are the only things that differ from what you pushed.

**`main` changed `isFileStale` out from under this PR.** It is now synchronous and takes only a path — the `IxClient` parameter is gone, because it reads a local baseline file rather than the patch log, and it is called per result on `explain`/`locate`/`read` where the `await` used to cost a backend round-trip per file.

Your version still had the old `async (client, filePath)` shape, so the two collided head-on. I kept **`main`'s signature** and **your behaviour**:

```ts
export function isFileStale(filePath: string): boolean {
  const workspaceRoot = path.resolve(resolveWorkspaceRoot());
  const baseline = loadIngestBaseline(workspaceRoot);
  if (!baseline) return false;

  const absolutePath = path.isAbsolute(filePath)
    ? path.resolve(filePath)
    : path.resolve(workspaceRoot, filePath);
  if (!fs.existsSync(absolutePath)) {
    return baseline.files.has(absolutePath) || baseline.files.has(filePath);
  }
  ...
}
```

So your two changes survive — the workspace-relative path resolution, and a deleted-but-mapped file reporting stale instead of the old blanket `if (!fs.existsSync) return false`. That early return was the bug this PR exists to fix, and dropping it is what `main`'s rewrite would otherwise have quietly undone.

The matching test edits: `stale-workspace.test.ts` dropped the now-unused `IxClient` import and the `noPatchReads` stub, and the three call sites in your new "reports a mapped file that was deleted after ingest" case moved to the sync signature.

Verified after the rebase: `npm run typecheck` clean, full `ix-cli` suite **693 passed** (52 files), `patchBuilder.test.ts` passing, lint 0 errors / 38 pre-existing warnings. All four `isFileStale` callers already use the sync form.

The review feedback above is unchanged — in particular the map-mode chunk deletion, which is still the blocking item.
